<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deir el-Bahari</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deir el-Bahari is a bay of cliffs on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, site of the mortuary temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a> of the 11th Dynasty and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, whose terraced temple bears reliefs of the expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>. In the tomb DB320 nearby, priests of the 21st Dynasty hid royal mummies, which were found in 1881.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari.jpg" alt="Deir el-Bahari" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nowic talk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tempel_der_Hatschepsut_(Deir_el-Bahari).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Deir el-Bahari, also called Dayr al-Bahri, is a complex of mortuary temples and tombs on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, and part of the Theban Necropolis. Its Arabic name means the Northern Monastery, and its Coptic name refers to the monastery of Apa Phoibammon. Hathor was a prominent goddess there, and the cliffs were sacred to her before pharaonic construction. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, supreme god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anubis">Anubis</a> were also honoured in its temples and chapels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its history begins with the 11th Dynasty king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a>, who built a funerary temple on several levels in the great bay to commemorate the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>. Its terraced plan, with a royal tomb built into the platform, pioneered the cliffside temple style. The shrines and tombs of royal wives and daughters, who served as priestesses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>, stood along the west wall of the terrace.</p><p>Centuries later the 18th Dynasty queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> had her temple, Djeser-Djeseru, built beside it, designed by her steward and architect Senenmut. It rises in colonnaded terraces to 30 metres, and its reliefs recite the divine birth of the queen and an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Land of Punt</a>. Some of her statues were destroyed in a posthumous condemnation of her, and others were rededicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>. He added a smaller temple above hers, Djeser-Akhet. In the Ptolemaic and Roman periods Hatshepsut's temple became a cult centre for the deified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a> and Amenhotep, son of Hapu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In the Third Intermediate Period the site became a burial ground for priests. A tomb in a recess of the cliffs, known as TT320 or DB320, held a cache of forty royal mummies moved from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a> by priests of the 21st Dynasty, most likely to prevent looting. They included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmose-i">Ahmose I</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. It was found in 1881. A second cache of 153 priests' mummies came to light in 1891 at Bab el-Gasus.</p><p>Édouard Naville excavated for the Egypt Exploration Fund from 1893 to 1906, and Herbert Winlock led the Metropolitan Museum of Art's expeditions from 1911 to 1931. Polish missions have undertaken major conservation work since the 1960s and rediscovered the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>. The site belongs to the UNESCO World Heritage listing Ancient Thebes with its Necropolis.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deir el-Bahari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>She sent the celebrated expedition to Punt and sponsored a large building programme at Karnak and <b>Deir el-Bahari</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The best-known record, the reliefs of Hatshepsut's temple at <b>Deir el-Bahari</b> (c. 1470 BCE), show houses on stilts, domed huts, a ruler called Parahu and his queen Ati, and myrrh trees being loaded in baskets for replanting in Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/land-of-punt">Land of Punt</a></li><li><blockquote>His mortuary temple at <b>Deir el-Bahari</b> set a model for later Theban royal building.</blockquote><a href="/state/theban-eleventh-dynasty">Theban Eleventh Dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Faced with tomb robbery, the priests moved the royal mummies to caches such as <b>Deir el-Bahari</b> tomb DB320, where many were found in 1881.</blockquote><a href="/state/theocracy-of-amun-at-thebes">Theocracy of Amun at Thebes</a></li></ul></section>
